Chapter: IdentLetters
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 An identifier may carry letters from scripts other than English, which is
 what "any language can be used in the source" has to mean at the level of a
 name. The lexer already accepted multi-byte characters through their own
 Tier 1 path; what it refused was the THIRTY-ONE letters that Tier 0 carries
 in one byte, because is-letter's band stopped at 64 and those sit at
 97..127.

 The names below are chosen from exactly that band. The sixteen accented
 Latin letters CCE carries in Tier 0 are e-acute, e-grave, e-circumflex,
 e-diaeresis, a-acute, a-grave, a-circumflex, a-diaeresis, o-acute,
 o-circumflex, o-diaeresis, u-acute, u-diaeresis, n-tilde, c-cedilla and
 i-acute; the fifteen Cyrillic are a o e i n t s r v l k m d p u. A letter
 outside that set is a Tier 1 character and takes the other path, so it would
 prove nothing about this band.

 This file will not compile against a seed built before 2026-07-27: the
 identifiers are error tokens there, which is what makes it evidence rather
 than decoration.

Section: Names

  café : Integer = 3

  año : Integer = 4

  дом : Integer = 5

  suma-café-año : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  suma-café-año (a) (b) = a + b

 Case conversion is deliberately not exercised here. The uppercase of these
 letters is a Tier 1 code point and to-upper answers a Char, so the identity
 is the honest answer and it is asserted by build/oracle-cce.ps1 rather than
 by a name.

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (show (suma-café-año café año))
    print-line-uni (show дом)
  end

